WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.402
-Guy, thank you so much

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:05.138
for coming back to the show
and coming on.

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:07.306
-Thank you for having me.
It's great to see you.

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:09.308
Seeing you is like --
it's like going back in time

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:10.877
to a better time.

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.612
'Cause the last time I saw you
was a better time.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.347
-It actually was.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:17.483
But you still continue
to crush it.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:21.854
I want to get into your shows,
but also congrats on the book.

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:23.923
Number one.
Not too shabby, buddy.

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.458
-Thank you.
-I don't know how you

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.794
had time to write a book, but
you're putting out new episodes

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.531
of "How I Built This"
and you started a live version

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:33.399
I want to talk about,
the "Resilience" series.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:35.601
How did this come about?
Was it your idea?

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.338
Did you go like, "Hey this is
a perfect time to talk to

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:39.839
these entrepreneurs and CEOs

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.707
about what they're doing
during COVID?

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:43.576
-Once the pandemic hit,
it was clear that

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.911
our community needed
a gathering place,

00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:47.914
because a lot of people who
listen to the show are

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.549
entrepreneurs, or they have
small businesses.

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:52.384
And people were and still are
freaking out.

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:55.655
And so we wanted to create
a place where

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.723
we could bring on
well-known entrepreneurs,

00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:00.693
from like Airbnb and Lyft
and, you know, and Slack,

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.496
and talk to our audience
and basically describe

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.264
the challenges
they're facing, too.

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:07.600
So in a way we wanted to
sort of say, "Look, you know,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.836
you're not the only one,
and here's how other people

00:01:10.836 --> 00:01:12.938
are building resilience,
and here are some of

00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:14.740
the creative ways they're
actually keeping

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.543
their businesses alive,
and it's been amazing.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.878
We're doing a video podcast
twice a week.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.514
We've done it since early April.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:24.316
And it's been so fun.
And we take live questions.

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:25.785
It's been amazing.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.555
Let's talk about your new book.
It just announced that it's

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.589
on the "New York Times"
bestseller's list,

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.958
and hit number one on
"The Wall Street Journal's"

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:35.828
best-selling business books.
Congrats, buddy.

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:37.296
-Thank you.
-I love it.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:41.000
I don't want it to end.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.102
I'm thanking you for
autographing it for me,

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.337
by the way.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.005
I appreciate that. Here.
I have it right here.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.173
-You're welcome.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.909
-"Jimmy, hope you like book.
Guy."

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:52.044
I mean, your handwriting
is different

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:54.313
than what I thought it would be,
but interesting.

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:57.383
"Hope you like book."
Well, that's interesting.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.352
Uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.520
When you're putting this
together, did you go back

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.822
and listen to all the interviews
you've done?

00:02:03.822 --> 00:02:07.426
I mean, it seems intense.

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.729
-Yeah, I mean, I interview
people for three or four

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.164
or sometimes five hours.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:13.833
We only put an hour
on the show.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.868
But really what was amazing,

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:18.571
and the insight
I really walked away from

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:22.241
after really trying to put
the book together,

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:24.744
was that entrepreneurship is
actually a mindset.

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:27.413
It's a way of thinking.
It's a way of operating.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.416
It's a series of skills
that you can learn.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:33.619
It's solving problems.
It's overcoming obstacles.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.122
It's dealing with the fear
of rejection.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.457
And the only difference between
the people that I profile

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.759
in the book and,
you know, the rest of us,

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:43.396
is that they walked into the
phone booth and put on the cape,

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.831
and the idea behind the book,
really,

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:48.834
is to be the phone booth
for people who are ready

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:50.870
to put on the cape.
-Yeah. Anyone can do it.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.805
It's just -- they didn't have
any special skills

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.474
that you don't have.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.208
You know, you talk about
something in the book

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:59.745
called the founder's dilemma,
which I find very interesting.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:02.448
This is when a company
becomes a success,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:05.384
and the founder has to
choose between

00:03:05.384 --> 00:03:07.553
having his company,
having control of the company,

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.589
or selling it
for a lot of money.

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:10.723
-Right.

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:12.391
-But you say that there's
a third option.

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:13.859
-There is, right?

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:16.395
And it sounds very simple,
but it's happiness.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.564
Like, here's the thing, Jimmy.

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:21.033
The vast majority, I would say
virtually every person

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:22.167
who's been on
"How I Built This,"

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.003
whether it's Howard Schultz
of Starbucks

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:26.606
or Payal Kadakia
who founded Classpass,

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:29.942
they don't go into starting
a business to become rich.

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:31.610
They go in to solve a problem.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.646
Because a business essentially,
that's what it is.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:36.382
It's, there's a problem,
and I'm going to solve it

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:37.850
with a product or service.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.419
Right?
-Yeah.

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:40.619
-So Howard Schultz
wanted to solve it

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.621
by brewing better coffee
and Payal Kadakia

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:45.424
wanted to solve it
by creating a more efficient way

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:48.694
to sign up for fitness classes.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:51.965
The reality is many of the
founders I've interviewed

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:55.101
who have sold their businesses
too early

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.869
have walked away with
a lot of money,

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:59.505
but in many cases
it hasn't left them happier

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.274
because what really motivates
them is the camaraderie

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:06.411
of a team, is a sense of
purpose, is a mission, right?

00:04:06.411 --> 00:04:09.148
And so that's what gets us out
of bed in the morning.

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.650
It's not having piles of money.
It's having a mission.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.019
It's having a sense of purpose.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:15.855
It's having a reason
to take on the world.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.491
And I found that again and again
with founders,

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:20.626
and I think that's
really what makes

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.361
entrepreneurship so special

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:25.331
is that the vast majority of
people who go into it

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:26.999
don't do it for the money.

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:30.369
-Who is the founder
of Dropbox?

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:32.604
-Drew Houston is
a great example.

00:04:32.604 --> 00:04:34.273
He's going to be on the show
in a couple weeks.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:36.442
I mean, he is worth
billions of dollars.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.911
-Yeah.
-He didn't go into it

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:42.815
wanting to, knowing he was ever
going to be a billionaire.

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.883
And to him it's just
a number on paper.

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:47.420
But I asked him in my interview
recently, you know,

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.222
"What do you think --"
He is only 37 years old.

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:51.023
"What do you think about
the future?

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:52.959
Do you think you'd want to sell
and walk away

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.128
and, you know, kind of just
live your life

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:57.630
sipping margaritas
on the beach?"

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.099
And he said, "You know, there's
no way I would want to do that,"

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.101
for the exact reasons
I just described --

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:03.735
because there's a mission,
there's a team,

00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:05.938
there's a reason, there are
problems to solve.

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.741
And that's what motivates him.
That's what gets him excited.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.477
-I just want to say please keep
doing what you're doing

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.546
because we need it right now
more than ever.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.682
Guy Raz, everybody.
His book "How I Built This"

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:18.317
is available now,
and when you come back

00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:21.387
I really want an actual signed
copy if you don't mind.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.088
-I promise.
-Guy Raz, everybody.

00:05:23.088 --> 00:05:24.957
We'll be right back with
a performance

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.826
from Lele Pons and Guaynaa.
Stick around.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.861
♪♪

